INT. GRAND HOTEL LAUNDRY ROOM - NIGHT

Steam hisses from industrial presses. Carts of white linen. A tall window shows the city's lights.

CROWE (50s, quiet, hotel uniform) pushes a cart down the aisle, the picture of a bored night porter. Behind him, two figures in identical gray overalls follow: IMOGEN TATE (30s), who counts keys, and PIOTR SELENKO (40s), who whistles.

CROWE
(to the group)
We walk. We do not run. We do not look at the cameras.

PIOTR
(under his breath)
Which cameras?

IMOGEN
Piotr.

PIOTR
Sorry.

They reach a service lift. Crowe presses the call button. Nothing.

PIOTR (CONT'D)
Is it supposed to be broken?

CROWE
It is supposed to be slow.

The lift arrives. They ride up in silence. Crowe checks a pocket watch. Imogen clicks her tongue against her teeth.

IMOGEN
(to herself)
Two hundred and six. Two hundred and seven.

PIOTR
What are you counting?

IMOGEN
Seconds.

Ding. The doors open on the penthouse corridor. The carpet is red, the walls gold. A single GUARD (50s, hotel blazer) sits in an armchair at the far end, asleep.

CROWE
(to the group)
Gentle. Gentle.

They creep past the guard. Piotr, passing, nudges a vase with his elbow; it wobbles; he catches it, grinning like a boy. Imogen swallows a laugh.

Crowe produces a key. The suite door opens without a sound. Inside: a vault door, humming softly, gleaming. Nobody speaks.

Then every light in the corridor comes on at once. The guard stands, no longer asleep, and unholsters a pistol.

GUARD
Welcome, gentlemen. And lady.

Crowe's shoulders sag a fraction. He slowly raises his hands.

CROWE
Evening, Marcus.

The Guard smiles, and now we see the badge on his belt: HOTEL SECURITY, DIRECTOR.

MARCUS
You were always punctual.
